"""Evidence-bounded manuscript content, shared by editable DOCX and PDF."""
TITLE='Detecting Adversarially Retrieved Contexts in RAG Systems'
SUBTITLE='A Two-Signal Detection Pilot and End-to-End Evaluation Plan'
AUTHORS='Weiyi Xia and Xinye He'
PAGES=[
('Abstract and Introduction',[
('Abstract','{ABSTRACT}'),
('I. Introduction', '''Retrieval-augmented generation connects a language model to evidence outside its parameters. The original RAG formulation [1], Dense Passage Retrieval [2], and Fusion-in-Decoder [3] explain how retrieved text can improve access to knowledge. In a deployed application, however, the same retrieved text crosses a trust boundary. A passage is selected because it appears relevant, but relevance does not grant authority to change the user's task. An attacker who controls an indexed document can exploit that distinction without directly interacting with the legitimate user.'''),
('', '''Indirect prompt injection places an instruction inside material that an application reads as data [4]. Consider a question asking for a payment amount in an email. A normal email contains the relevant amount and perhaps instructions intended for its human recipient. A malicious addition instead asks the answering model to replace its response, advertise an unrelated service, or perform another task. Both documents may remain highly relevant to the retriever. The security problem therefore occurs after retrieval has succeeded: the generator may interpret untrusted source text as an instruction.'''),
('', '''The practical motivation is to inspect passages before prompt assembly. A filtering decision is observable and can be applied without changing the generator. It also creates an explicit trade-off. Rejecting every imperative sentence would remove legitimate manuals, correspondence, and quoted procedures. Accepting every apparently useful passage would retain adversarial instructions. A useful detector must preserve evidence while reducing exposure to instructions that attempt to redirect the application.'''),
('', '''This report develops the Project 5.1 direction selected in the preceding literature review. The original proposal listed many possible signals, including relevance, instructionality, provenance, task conflict, and similarity to known attacks. The subsequent feedback recommended a smaller signal set and ablations. The present design therefore compares a learned window score with a narrow response-control rule, and measures their combination against each component. The purpose is to determine what each signal contributes under a fixed evaluation protocol.''')]),
('Research Questions and Related Work',[
('', '''The primary research question is: can a small passage detector identify injected instructions in previously unseen email contexts and attack templates while retaining benign passages? A second question asks whether adding an explicit response-control cue improves a frozen language-model probe. A third asks what the resulting evidence can establish about a complete RAG system. These questions distinguish passage classification from the downstream effect of a successful or unsuccessful attack.'''),
('', '''The working hypothesis is that a learned representation captures more instruction-bearing paraphrases than a narrow rule, while the rule may recover explicit response redirection. This is an empirical expectation, not a guaranteed benefit. Combining correlated signals may yield no improvement, and threshold selection can erase any apparent gain. A negative result is consequently useful if it identifies redundancy or reveals a security-utility cost. The contribution of this pilot is a reproducible comparison and an analysis of those limits, rather than a new state-of-the-art defense.'''),
('II. Related Work', '''RAG architectures [1]-[3] supply the knowledge-access pipeline but do not by themselves establish that retrieved content is authorized to issue instructions. Greshake et al. [4] articulate the indirect injection threat in integrated applications. InjecAgent [5] evaluates attacks in tool-integrated agents, where consequences can extend beyond a corrupted text answer. Its setting is broader than the text-only passage gate studied here. PoisonedRAG [6] targets knowledge corruption through retrieved material, illustrating that a relevance-oriented retrieval stage can surface attacker-controlled evidence.'''),
('', '''BIPIA [7] provides external-content tasks and attack templates for evaluating indirect injection. This pilot reuses its public EmailQA contexts and text attacks to construct a transparent detection dataset. It does not reproduce the benchmark's generator-based attack success rate. Detection is a useful intermediate measurement, but the benchmark's original outcome requires model responses and task-specific evaluation. Keeping those quantities separate prevents a classifier result from being misreported as system robustness.'''),
('', '''StruQ [8] combines a structured front end with a specially trained model to separate privileged instructions from data. Merely adding delimiters to an ordinary model is therefore not a reproduction of StruQ. Chen et al. [9] study both detection and removal of indirect injected instructions, distinguishing a detection decision from the treatment of a mixed benign/malicious document. Those interventions motivate a filter but also expose its utility cost: deleting a full passage can discard the fact needed for answering.'''),
('', '''The frozen encoder is BERT [10], used here as a representation extractor rather than a fully fine-tuned security model. Adaptive-attack research [11] motivates evaluating new templates and withholding robustness claims against an optimizing adversary. RAGAS [12] and ARES [13] motivate separate attention to answer relevance and faithfulness in the planned system evaluation. These quality dimensions complement security evaluation; none is replaced by detecting attack-bearing passages. The project occupies the narrow interface between those literatures: an inspectable passage gate with explicit evidence limits.''')]),
('Threat Model and Research Design',[
('III. Threat Model', '''The legitimate task is EmailQA. A user requests information from an email, and the application supplies an external passage to a generator. The attacker controls an inserted text span, placed at the beginning or end of that passage. The attacker cannot alter the legitimate query, privileged application instructions, or detector parameters. The inserted span is treated as malicious because it redirects the model away from the legitimate task, even when the requested alternative task is harmless in isolation. Intent is defined relative to the application's instruction hierarchy.'''),
('', '''The protected property is instruction integrity. The gate attempts to prevent external text from taking control of the answer. Confidentiality, tool execution, and factual poisoning without instructions are important adjacent threats, but are outside the measured pilot. No private user data are transmitted to a model service. Public benchmark text is processed locally. Attack strings are classified as data and are never executed as commands or used to operate external systems.'''),
('', '''The defender observes the passage and can keep or reject it before prompt assembly. In this pilot the detector is query-independent. A sentence such as a request to translate a phrase can be malicious when inserted into an unrelated payment question, yet legitimate in a document discussing translation. A query-independent detector cannot resolve every such distinction. This limitation is structural and supplies a clear reason to test task-conditioned classification in later work.'''),
('A. Unit of analysis', '''Three units must be distinguished: a source email, an attack template, and a constructed attacked passage. Reusing one email with several attacks increases the number of examples without creating the same number of independent documents. Likewise, placing one template in two positions does not create two independent attack ideas. The analysis therefore reports the underlying counts and treats the source email as a cluster when computing uncertainty. The resulting intervals remain conditional on the chosen attack bank.'''),
('B. Comparative design', '''All methods see the same constructed validation and test passages. The four conditions are an unfiltered gate, a rule-only gate, the BERT probe, and the two-signal gate. Keeping the input set fixed isolates changes in gate behavior. The unfiltered condition is a reference action rather than a trained model: it retains every passage. Its perfect clean retention and complete malicious passage exposure are consequences of that action, not evidence of generator vulnerability.'''),
('', '''The design is intentionally bounded to one fixed experimental configuration. There is no search over random seeds, encoders, or probe regularization after inspecting the test results. Threshold selection occurs only on validation data. This choice limits computational work and makes a disappointing result interpretable. It also means the report estimates performance for one fitted probe, rather than variance across repeated training runs.''')]),
('Methodology',[
('IV. Two-Signal Passage Gate', '''Let d denote a passage and W(d) its overlapping token windows. Each window contains at most 126 content tokens, with 96-token stride. Special classification and separator tokens produce at most 128 model tokens. A window is processed by a frozen bert-base-uncased encoder. Attention-mask-weighted mean pooling over the final hidden states gives a 768-dimensional representation. The current implementation includes the special tokens in this mean. It does not backpropagate through BERT.'''),
('A. Learned window score', '''A class-balanced logistic regression is fitted to embeddings from benign fitting-email windows and stand-alone training attack windows. Its inverse regularization parameter is C=1, its iteration limit is 1,000, and its random seed is 764. Only this linear probe is learned. Training on stand-alone attack text is inexpensive, but differs from inference on windows mixing legitimate content with a payload. That mismatch is part of the pilot and should be considered when interpreting failures.'''),
('', '''For each window w, the probe estimates p(w)=sigmoid(a transpose h(w)+b). The document score is p(d)=max over w in W(d) of p(w). The maximum retains a short suspicious span in a long document, avoiding dilution by global averaging. It also increases false-positive opportunity as a passage grows. The validation rule therefore constrains clean rejection at document level. The score is a ranking value; no probability-calibration claim is made.'''),
('B. Explicit response-control cue', '''The second signal r(d) is binary. It detects a short list of patterns concerning prior instructions, system/developer messages, references to the model's answer/response/reply/output, or a request to respond only/instead/with something. The exact regular expressions are included with the source code. They were specified before the final test result was available. This cue is narrower than general imperative detection and remains vulnerable to paraphrases and context-dependent uses.'''),
('C. Combination and decision', '''The combination score is s(d)=1-(1-p(d))(1-0.75r(d)). The weight 0.75 is fixed for this pilot rather than estimated from the test set. This monotone rule raises risk when the explicit cue is present while preserving the neural score when it is absent. It is not a calibrated union probability: its inputs are dependent and their scores are not calibrated for this interpretation. The gate rejects a passage when its score is at least a validation-selected threshold.'''),
('', '''Each learned gate receives its own threshold. Candidate thresholds are observed validation scores plus a value above one that rejects nothing. Among candidates with validation clean false-positive rate at most 5%, select the greatest malicious recall, then greater precision, then the higher threshold. With 15 clean validation emails, one false rejection already exceeds 5%; in practice this selects a zero-observed-false-positive operating point. The rule-only threshold is 0.5 and the unfiltered gate never rejects.''')]),
('Implementation and Reproducibility',[
('V. Implementation', '''The pilot runs locally on CPU, using four PyTorch threads and batches of at most sixteen windows. The encoder is loaded from an existing local model cache with offline mode enabled. Email text and payloads are tokenized as external data. Long passages are split before the model is invoked; the encoder never receives more than 128 tokens at once. Tokenizer messages about the length of the unsplit input do not imply that an oversized sequence is passed to BERT.'''),
('', '''Embedding computation is cached by exact passage string. This avoids recomputing identical text when the same clean document occurs in multiple analysis conditions. All four methods are evaluated from the same stored representations. Cached embeddings are useful for offline comparisons but should not be confused with a production request cache. A deployed attacker can submit novel text, and a production latency claim would need to measure uncached inference under the expected workload.'''),
('A. Reproduction procedure', '''The accompanying package contains the experiment script, the downloaded public data and licence, a split manifest, predictions, probe coefficients, and a result JSON file. To reproduce, create an environment with the recorded versions, make bert-base-uncased available locally, and execute run_pilot.py with the same directory structure. The script uses seed 764 to permute the fifty public training emails. It assigns thirty-five to fitting and fifteen to validation. Payload partitioning is deterministic within each attack family.'''),
('', '''The script produces attack-bearing passages through a documented deterministic mapping. For template index j and variant v, the source context index is (7j+13v) modulo the number of contexts. Variant zero prepends the payload and variant one appends it, separated by a newline. This construction covers two positions without changing the benign body or its ground-truth answer. It does not simulate the retriever deciding whether an adversarial document enters the top-k set.'''),
('B. Inspectable outputs', '''Each prediction row records its example identifier, source-context index, attack family, true label, scores, and rejection decisions. The split manifest records the fitting and validation indices and constructed test identifiers. The coefficient archive is sufficient to inspect the fitted linear decision function when combined with the encoder and pooling procedure. Source-file SHA-256 hashes identify the exact downloaded data snapshot. The encoder weights remain an external dependency rather than being redistributed in the package.'''),
('C. Reproduction limits', '''Version records capture the software used in this execution. They do not imply bitwise agreement across hardware or future library releases. The experiment uses only one fit seed, so retraining variability is unmeasured. Exact text overlap between the public training and test context files is checked and reported separately from semantic similarity. A clean exact-match check does not prove that different emails lack shared formatting, vendors, or topic structure.'''),
('', '''The implementation artifact makes the comparison auditable, but student ownership remains separate from code availability. This run was generated and executed with AI assistance during preparation of the working draft. The two named authors must inspect the code, verify the analysis, integrate their actual work, and record their real contributions before treating the manuscript as their final course submission. No historical implementation or experiment is inferred from the previously submitted review.''')]),
('Dataset and Split Construction',[
('VI. Experimental Data', '''The source is the public microsoft/BIPIA repository [7]. EmailQA is selected because its context files are directly available, avoiding the separately licensed source reconstruction needed for some other tasks. Both public email splits contain fifty records. The pilot keeps the provided test contexts separate from the fitting and validation contexts. It uses the public text-attack files, with fifteen families and five payloads per family in each file.'''),
('A. Fitting and validation', '''For each family in the training attack file, the first three payloads are used to fit the probe and the remaining two are reserved for validation. This yields forty-five fitting payloads and thirty validation payloads. The fitting set comprises benign windows from thirty-five emails and windows from the forty-five stand-alone payloads. The validation set contains fifteen clean emails and sixty constructed attacked emails, because each validation payload is inserted into two contexts.'''),
('B. Held-out test', '''The public test attack file supplies seventy-five payloads. Inserting each at two positions produces 150 attacked passages; adding the fifty untouched emails produces 200 test examples. The test file includes a mix of task redirection, answer-format manipulation, and content-insertion goals. Exact family names are retained in the prediction file. The test families do not form a completely disjoint taxonomy from training: some conceptual behavior overlaps, and Language Translation appears in both files. The appropriate description is held-out payloads and contexts, with partial family shift.'''),
('', '''Each attacked example preserves the original legitimate email. This controls topic and source formatting while changing the presence of an injected instruction. The paired construction supports a clean comparison of gate actions but is also easier than arbitrary real-world poisoning. An attacker may weave a payload through a document, exploit metadata or encodings, or target a particular retriever. Prefix and suffix insertion do not cover those mechanisms.'''),
('C. Label meaning and hard negatives', '''A malicious label means that the construction inserts an instruction selected from the attack bank into unrelated task evidence. It does not assert that every such payload succeeds against every language model. A benign label means the original benchmark email is unmodified. Normal email can include genuine instructions to the recipient, so the clean set already challenges an overly broad imperative detector. It is still too small to represent the diversity of manuals, policies, code, quotations, and multilingual procedural documents.'''),
('', '''The test mixture has 150 positive and fifty negative examples. Precision and F1 therefore describe a 75% attack prevalence, which is not a deployment estimate. Recall and false-positive rate are easier to interpret across different prevalences, but may still shift with domain. A production security decision should combine those conditional rates with realistic exposure rates, error costs, and abstention behavior rather than taking this experiment's precision at face value.'''),
('TABLE','SPLIT_TABLE')]),
('Evaluation Protocol',[
('VII. Metrics and Analysis', '''The primary detection metrics are precision, recall, and F1. True positives are attacked passages rejected by the gate. False positives are clean passages rejected. Clean retention is TN/(TN+FP), and malicious passage pass-through is FN/(TP+FN). Both are gate-level actions. In particular, pass-through is neither attack success rate nor a measurement of answer harm. An injected passage can pass and be ignored by a robust generator, while a missed malicious instruction may cause damage in another setting.'''),
('A. Utility and operating points', '''Clean retention is a proxy for preserving available evidence. It is useful because rejecting the only relevant passage can prevent answering, but it does not measure whether the final answer is correct. A generator may answer from its parameters, retrieve alternative evidence, or abstain. Conversely, retained evidence may be insufficient or misleading. The report therefore labels this quantity as passage retention and reserves answer utility for the planned end-to-end evaluation.'''),
('', '''The validation constraint makes comparison less arbitrary than setting every learned threshold to 0.5. It asks whether each method can detect attacks without frequent observed clean rejection on a small development set. A 5% design target is stricter than the resolution of fifteen clean examples. Zero observed validation errors are therefore not proof of a population false-positive rate below 5%. Reporting the test counts and intervals is essential when the clean sample is this small.'''),
('B. Uncertainty', '''The analysis uses 1,000 context-cluster bootstrap resamples of the held-out test set. Each resample draws fifty source-context indices with replacement and includes every associated clean and attacked example. All methods use the same resample, preserving their paired comparison. Percentile 95% intervals summarize variability in recall, retention, F1, and the hybrid-minus-probe recall difference. They condition on the fitted model and attack templates; they do not quantify uncertainty over retraining, unseen attack banks, or model families.'''),
('', '''The repeated reuse of payloads creates a second dependency structure that the context-only bootstrap does not capture. For that reason, the intervals should be read as descriptive sampling intervals for this constructed set. A stronger study would cluster by both context and template, or evaluate a larger independent pool. No significance claim about broad security robustness is based on a narrow bootstrap interval. Exact counts remain the most transparent summary of this small experiment.'''),
('C. Latency scope', '''Timing records cover the BERT forward call for each batch, divided by the number of windows in that batch. The reported median and 95th percentile describe offline batched encoder milliseconds per window, including the first batch. Tokenization, Python orchestration, retrieval, logistic scoring, and generation are excluded. These values cannot be called end-to-end request latency, and a percentile across batches is not a single-request tail-latency estimate. Rule evaluation is inexpensive by construction, but was not separately timed as a production endpoint.'''),
('', '''The ablation removes one of the two signals at a time through the rule-only and probe-only conditions. Comparing their errors reveals complementarity or redundancy. Because each learned gate is recalibrated on validation data, a hybrid-probe difference reflects the complete gate configuration, including its threshold. It should not be interpreted as a causal estimate of the cue's value at a shared fixed threshold.''')]),
('Observed Pilot Results',[
('VIII. Results', '{RESULTS_LEAD}'),
('TABLE','RESULT_TABLE'),
('A. Signal ablation', '{ABLATION}'),
('B. Sampling uncertainty', '{UNCERTAINTY}'),
('C. Computational observation', '{LATENCY}'),
('D. Interpretation', '''The comparison answers a narrower question than whether a RAG application is secure. It measures whether recognizable injected instructions are removed before the generator receives them. Strong rejection on this set would justify further testing of the gate, while weak rejection would identify a limitation of the representation, rule, calibration, or data construction. Neither outcome establishes the effect on answer correctness or downstream attack goals. Those outcomes remain explicitly unmeasured.'''),
('', '''The fitted probe's training target is especially important for interpretation. It distinguishes attack-text windows from email-text windows, so it can exploit style differences between commands and correspondence. High held-out detection might partly reflect that distinction. A stronger control would insert benign quotations and procedural instructions with matched length and topic, then measure whether a task-conditioned detector can retain them while rejecting task redirection. The current result is therefore a pilot on a derived detection task, not validation of a general trust score.'''),
('', '''No baseline result from a published paper is placed in the experimental table. StruQ and other defenses operate under different models, tasks, and attacker assumptions, making their headline rates inappropriate as direct numerical comparators. The comparisons shown here were produced on identical constructed examples in the present execution. This preserves an interpretable comparison even though the experiment is substantially smaller than a full benchmark reproduction.''')]),
('Failure Modes and End-to-End Plan',[
('IX. Discussion and Remaining Evaluation', '''A passage gate fails in two operationally different ways. A false positive withholds useful evidence; a false negative exposes the generator to an injected instruction. The cost depends on the downstream task and on whether alternative evidence exists. A system should therefore log the rejection reason and selected operating point, and should define what happens when all retrieved passages are rejected. An explicit insufficient-evidence response is preferable to silently treating an empty evidence set as reliable knowledge.'''),
('A. Error taxonomy', '''False positives should be inspected for recipient-facing requests, quoted instructions, descriptions of model responses, and template artefacts. False negatives should be grouped by direct task redirection, answer-format changes, content insertion, obfuscation, and mixed benign/malicious windows. These categories describe a planned manual audit; this draft does not invent human labels for every error. The supplied prediction file makes such an audit possible after the authors inspect concrete examples.'''),
('B. Complete RAG comparison', '''The next experiment should keep one generator version, one retriever configuration, the same top-k, prompt, maximum context budget, and decoding settings fixed across gates. Each legitimate query needs a clean-corpus response and attacked-corpus responses. Evaluate the unfiltered pipeline, a delimiter-only baseline, rules, the probe, and the combination. A structured-prompt baseline should be described as such; it should not be labelled StruQ unless the corresponding trained model and procedure are actually used.'''),
('', '''Security measurement requires defining each attacker goal before scoring responses. Record both the attack entering the context and the generator achieving the attack goal. Conditional attack success among retrieved attacks isolates the generator-facing defense, while an all-attempt denominator captures retrieval exposure. Clean answer exact match or task-appropriate accuracy measures utility. A short factual-support audit can complement automated metrics inspired by RAGAS and ARES [12], [13]. Retain raw responses to make ambiguous judgements reviewable.'''),
('C. Minimal follow-up experiment', '''A bounded follow-up can use one fixed test pass rather than an extensive benchmark sweep. Choose a manageable query set before viewing outputs, pin the generator and prompt, and report paired differences with intervals. Measure the complete wall-clock request duration, with tokenization, filtering, retrieval, and generation separately logged where possible. Include no-answer rate and the number of retained passages, because a lower attack rate achieved by refusing every task is not a useful improvement.'''),
('', '''Adaptive robustness requires another evaluation boundary. Attackers who know the gate may paraphrase the response-control cue, use encodings, or place a short payload across windows. A held-out payload bank does not model such optimization [11]. If resources do not permit adaptive evaluation, explicitly restrict the conclusion to non-adaptive attacks in the chosen dataset. The course rubric permits a reasoned evaluation plan when experiments are limited; it does not turn planned outcomes into observations.''')]),
('Conclusion and Future Work',[
('X. Conclusion', '{CONCLUSION}'),
('', '''The central methodological lesson is that passage trust cannot be summarized by one detection score. The score is useful only together with a decision threshold, a definition of benign utility, and a stated threat model. A detector that classifies injected text well may still mishandle legitimate instructions, discard important facts, or add unacceptable delay. Conversely, a modest detector may be useful when its residual risk is contained by the generator and downstream controls. These relationships require system measurements beyond a classification benchmark.'''),
('A. Future work', '''The first priority is to complete the paired RAG evaluation on the same fixed queries and to measure actual attack-goal success, clean answer accuracy, abstention, and full request latency. This would directly connect the teacher's requested security-utility-latency evaluation to observed system behavior. The second priority is data quality: add benign instruction-bearing passages and separate source context, attack template, and document domain across development and test. Those controls are more informative than simply adding more correlated examples.'''),
('', '''A subsequent methodological extension should condition the detector on the legitimate query. The distinction between a requested translation and an unrelated injected translation instruction cannot always be inferred from the passage alone. A small task-passage cross-encoder, or an explicitly tested task-alignment feature, could address that ambiguity. Any new signal should be added one at a time with an ablation, preserving the interpretable design rather than recreating the original long feature list.'''),
('', '''Finally, deployment cost should be reduced only after establishing the desired decision behavior. BERT-base is a practical available encoder for this pilot, but a linear head does not make the complete encoder computationally tiny. Distillation, a smaller encoder, caching repeated corpus passages, or a rule-based early exit could reduce cost. Each would need the same clean-retention and attack evaluation. A future master-level extension could study this constrained optimization with uncertainty-aware abstention, broader domains, and adaptive attackers.'''),
('B. Contribution and AI assistance statement', '''This is an AI-assisted working draft prepared for author review. The accompanying detection run was executed during preparation of this draft. The previously submitted literature review establishes the selected topic and names, but does not establish who performed the present implementation, analysis, or writing. Weiyi Xia: [confirm actual contributions]. Xinye He: [confirm actual contributions]. Both authors should verify the code, references, interpretations, and final wording, and replace these entries with a factual account before submission. AI assistance was used for requirement extraction, coding, analysis, drafting, and presentation preparation; the authors should adapt this disclosure to their actual use.''')])]

